{"kind":"request","text":"Builder: security, join and redemption hardening (simplification review 55563589, findings SEC-01, SEC-02, SEC-07). (1) A client-custody redemption mints a read session for whatever record submit returns, including an idempotent replay of a join copied from the log (packages/room/src/requests.ts:204-208; admission.ts earlySteps step 3 returns the stored record before authority, invitation state or custody are checked). Anyone who can read one signed join can obtain a one-hour renewable read session as that member. Issue a session only for a join this call committed; a replay returns no session. (2) A join refused at steps 7 to 9 through POST /acts is sealed with its full signed envelope, including body.secret (room.ts admits /acts with default hooks; admission.ts:218 skips recording only when recordRefusals is false; schema.ts exempts the secret from the secret scan), which contradicts R-GEN-6. Never record a refused join on any path, and reconcile R-ADM-8 with R-GEN-6 in the protocol. (3) The redemption rate limit is an in-memory map keyed by caller input before validation, never evicted, shared under one address for RPC callers, and not applied to joins on /acts (requests.ts:175-197; worker.ts:56). Validate the invitation ID before keying, bound or persist the counters, and apply the limit to joins on /acts. Tests: a replayed join through redeem yields no session; a refused join on /acts is unrecorded; the limit holds across /acts and /redeem.","body":{"conditions":"The three behaviours above are changed with tests that failed before the fix; docs/protocol.md amended where R-ADM-8 and R-GEN-6 conflict; root gates pass; checker review.","target_head":"a6330262b1a609843308d9a493dbd6132d428499","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}